  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 343 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n===== Gusty Winds and Showers Today and Sunday =====\n\nNow in the pre-dawn hours, winds are light with high level overcast \nskies streaming in from what's left of Isaias. Dry air already in \nplace from a surface high pressure will slowly drift eastwards. THis \nwill keep conditions at the surface dry until about mid morning when \nstrong moisture transport will try to overcome the low level RH \nvalues in the mid 50s. Despite the onset virga, PWATs will \neventually raise near 1.5\" or more by the afternoon, moistening the \natmospheric column enough for light to moderate banded rain showers \nat times. This will indicate the northern shield of Isaias moving \noverhead, with rain activity becoming more scattered or spotty by \nthe afternoon hours. Instability will be very little to none during \nthe day, meaning any thunder will be limited to non-existent. Wind \ngusts is of greater concern as the center of the remnants of Isaias \ndraws closer to KY. In tandem with the slowly departing high \npressure at lower levels, a pressure gradient will build, allowing \neasterly wind gusts of 30-40 mph at times, with sustained winds of \n15-20 mph, especially early in the afternoon. Throughout the \nafternoon and evening, wind gusts will gradually weaken as the \ncenter of the remnants of Isaias draws closer. In fact, the center \nof the low should move over our SE CWA. All the while, off-and-on \nrain showers will be present. Due to the persistence of cloud cover \nand rain showers, highs will struggle to get out of the 60s today. \nAny highs reaching the low 70s in the late morning will cool by the \nafternoon from the incoming moisture.\n\nSpeaking of, later Saturday night and into Sunday morning, a main \naxis of moisture will be draped directly over central KY. Aided by a \nstrong jet near 700mb, a few moderate rain showers could produce \nrain rates of near an inch an hour as this axis of moisture drifts \neast of I-75. Scattered rain showers will slowly begin drifting east \nas the center of Isaias departs to the east as well. Rainfall totals \nwill be lesser across our SW Indiana counties with higher totals \nacross the Bluegrass region. Generally anywhere from 0.5-1\" totals \nare expected, although isolated areas could receive 2-3\" across \ncentral KY and east of I-75 where that axis of increased moisture \nwill pivot as Isaias drifts away.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon and evening, clouds and showers will gradually \nclear from west to east, with warmer temperatures expected out west \nwith more exposure to the sun. Highs range from the upper 60s to \nlower 70s across the CWA.\n\n===== Next Week =====\n\nBeyond Sunday, a return to ridging in the middle of the country and \ntroughing in the west will allow a SW flow to return to our area. \nThis will boost highs unseasonably warm into the mid 80s across our \nwest with upper 70s across the east. As the ridging amplifies \nTuesday afternoon, a few places near the TN border will reach the \nupper 80s as the ridge keeps shortwaves of energy to far north to \ncool temperatures down. Eventually heading into Wednesday and days \nbeyond, the ridge will break down somewhat and our CWA gets caught \nin a battle of influence between strong passing lows over southern \nCanada, and continued ridging across the southern US. Recent trends \nhave suggested a stalling boundary draped over the Ohio Valley \nbringing low-end rain chances Thursday and Friday, whilst other \ntrends brings a more robust trough swinging from Canada by Friday \npushing out the southern ridge and a return to cooler air. For now, \nlow PoPs have been included in the forecast due to model spread with \nhighs in the 70s and 80s.",
